/**

@page page_exahype2_solver_manipulate_solver_steps Manipulate an existing solver's steps (e.g. inserting additional sweeps)

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE 2's numerical schemes do not hard-code a sequence of grid traversals.
Each scheme describes one time step as a task graph, and the task graph
compiler maps this graph onto grid sweeps later on. You can therefore alter
what a solver does per time step, e.g. postprocess its solution after each
update or add a step of your own, without touching the numerical scheme. You
modify the task graph before it is compiled.

This page describes the mechanism. The tutorial
@ref tutorials_exahype2_20_additional-solver-steps walks through a hands-on
example, a density floor for the Euler equations, for both Finite Volumes and
Runge-Kutta Finite Differences.


# From a solver to task graphs

Every exahype2.api.Solver contributes to three task graphs
(peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph), one per algorithmic phase: the setup of the
simulation, the time stepping, and the plotting. When the project generates
the code, it calls each solver's generate_solver_realisation(), which
proceeds in four steps:

1. The grid construction adds its tasks to the setup graph.
2. The numerical scheme adds its tasks to the time stepping graph.
3. The plotter, if any, adds its tasks to the plot graph.
4. The solver's postprocess_task_graphs() receives all three graphs.

Only afterwards, the project hands the graphs over to the task graph
compiler. postprocess_task_graphs() hence is the place where you can
manipulate a solver's steps: All tasks are known, but none of them has been
translated into a grid traversal yet.

The compiler decides how many grid traversals a graph needs. A task which
you add might be fused into an existing traversal, or it might require a
traversal of its own, i.e. an additional sweep. This depends on the data
that the task reads and writes, its dependencies and the compiler chosen. You
do not have to decide this yourself. However, you have to declare correctly
which data a task uses, as the compiler relies on these declarations.


# The tags Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag and Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag

To insert a step after a solution update, you have to find the tasks which
update the solution. You do not want to know how a particular numerical
scheme builds its graph, though. The schemes therefore label the relevant
tasks.

## Tags in general

Every peano4.api.taskgraph.Task carries a dictionary tags, which maps a key
onto an arbitrary value. Each key identifies one tag, so a task can carry
arbitrarily many tags at the same time:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
task = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(name="My task", entity=peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL, tags={"MyLabel": None})
task.tags[Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag] = Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag(solver, patch, guard)
task.tags[MyOwnTag]                     = MyOwnTag(...)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The task graph compilers ignore tags. They exist only so that the code which
builds a graph can tell other code what a task does.
peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph.find_tasks(tag) returns all tasks of a graph which
carry a certain key, in the order in which they have been added to the graph.
If you pass several keys, i.e. find_tasks(tag_a, tag_b), it returns all tasks
which carry at least one of them, each task once.

Use classes, rather than strings, as keys of your own tags. They cannot
clash with the tags of others by accident, and the value can be an instance
of the class which holds the information others need.

## The solution update tags

Each numerical scheme of ExaHyPE tags each task of its time stepping graph
which writes a new solution with exahype2.api.Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag.
Runge-Kutta schemes compute intermediate solutions, i.e. the inputs of further
stages, before they compute the new solution. They tag each task which writes
such an intermediate solution with
exahype2.api.Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag.

The key of a tag is its class itself. Its value is an instance of this class:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
task.tags[Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag]             = Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag(solver, patch, guard, guard_data)
task.tags[Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag] = Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag(solver, stage_input, guard, guard_data)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The distinction allows you to pick: A step which should act on the solution
only, e.g. some statistics, searches for SolutionUpdateTaskTag. A step which
should act on every intermediate solution, too, e.g. the enforcement of
constraints, searches for both tags.

IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag is a subclass of SolutionUpdateTaskTag.
Both hold the same four attributes:

- solver: The exahype2.api.Solver whose solution the task writes. A project
  with several solvers has several tagged tasks per graph, so filter for
  your own solver.
- patch: The cell data (peano4.api.datamodel.Patch) into which the task
  writes, i.e. the solution patch or, for an intermediate solution, the
  input of a Runge-Kutta stage. Your step has to work on this patch.
- guard: A C++ boolean expression which selects the cells that the task
  updates. Use the same guard in your step, so it touches only cells that
  have just been updated, and no cells of other solvers. The guard has to
  evaluate to the same value before and after the update.
- guard_data: A list of further cell data which the guard reads. Your task
  has to declare these data, too, as the generated code would not see them
  otherwise. The list is empty for most schemes.

## Which tasks are tagged

| Numerical scheme | Tag | Tagged tasks | Patch | Guard |
|------------------|-----|--------------|-------|-------|
| exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithExplicitEuler | SolutionUpdateTaskTag | the cell update | solution | the solver's cell guard |
| exahype2.api.fv.RusanovWithOptimisticExplicitEuler | SolutionUpdateTaskTag | the update of the whole patch (traditional branch) and the insertion of the precomputed patch interior (optimistic branch) | solution | the cell guard combined with the condition that picks the branch; guard_data holds the cell's eigenvalue and time step sizes, which this condition reads |
| exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta | IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag | "Prepare stage k+1" for every stage but the last one | the stage input | the solver's cell guard |
| exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta | SolutionUpdateTaskTag | "Complete time step" after the last stage | solution | the solver's cell guard |

The optimistic Finite Volumes compute the new solution in one of two tasks,
depending on the cell. Each of them is tagged with its own guard. A step that
you insert after both tasks hence runs once per cell and time step.

The Runge-Kutta scheme tags the writer of each stage. A step that you insert
after each task with either tag runs after each stage. Consequently, the
right-hand side of the next stage is evaluated on postprocessed data only.
This is what you need, e.g., to enforce constraints or a density floor, as an
unphysical stage could crash the flux evaluation. A step that you insert
after the tasks with SolutionUpdateTaskTag only runs once per time step.


# The contract

The tags are a contract between the numerical schemes and any code which
manipulates their graphs.

The numerical schemes promise to

- tag all tasks which write a new solution with SolutionUpdateTaskTag, and
  all tasks which write a new stage input with
  IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag; and
- not read the outcome of a writer within the writer's own task. A scheme
  must compute eigenvalues or the projection onto the faces in a subsequent
  task instead. This is why the Runge-Kutta scheme splits each stage into
  "Prepare stage k" and "Project stage k onto faces": Postprocessing has to
  fit in between, so that the neighbours see postprocessed data only.

Code which inserts steps has to

- declare all data that a new task reads or writes via use_cell(),
  use_face() or use_vertex(), including the tag's patch and guard_data;
- use the tag's guard;
- insert the task via peano4.api.taskgraph.Graph.insert_after() instead of
  adding a plain dependency.

insert_after(anchor, task) adds task to the graph and makes it depend on
anchor. In addition, it redirects all tasks which have depended on anchor so
far to the new task. Your task hence runs after the update but before
anything which used to follow the update, such as the projection onto the
faces. A plain task.add_dependency(anchor) would allow these successors to
run before your task, i.e. on data which has not been postprocessed yet.

If you insert several steps after the same anchor, chain them: use the task
which you have just inserted as the anchor of the next one.


# Inserting steps

## Point-wise postprocessing

Many steps alter each volume, or grid point, of a patch independently,
e.g. a density floor or the enforcement of an algebraic constraint. For
these, you do not have to touch the task graph yourself:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver.add_point_wise_postprocessing(
  name           = "Density floor",
  implementation = "Q[0] = std::max(Q[0], 0.9);",
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The snippet may read and write Q, i.e. all unknowns and auxiliary variables
of one volume, and read the volume's position x. The default implementation
of postprocess_task_graphs() searches the time stepping graph for all tasks
which carry this solver's SolutionUpdateTaskTag or
IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag. After each of them, it
inserts one task per postprocessing step, which runs the snippet via
toolbox.blockstructured.api.ApplyPointWiselyToPatch on the tag's patch with
the tag's guard. The task names read "{solver}_{name} after {task}". If you
run the generator with TEXT or VISUALISATION logging, it prints or plots the
time stepping graph including these tasks.

exagrype.api.CCZ4Solver relies on this mechanism: With
enforce_constraints=True, it adds exagrype::ccz4::enforceCCZ4constraints()
as point-wise postprocessing.

## Arbitrary steps

If a step does not fit the point-wise pattern, e.g. as it needs a halo or
works on faces or vertices, override postprocess_task_graphs() in a
subclass of exahype2.api.Solver. Call the superclass' implementation, so the
point-wise postprocessing remains in place:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
class MySolver(exahype2.api.Solver):
  def postprocess_task_graphs(self, exahype2_project, peano_project, setup_simulation, time_step, plot):
    super().postprocess_task_graphs(exahype2_project, peano_project, setup_simulation, time_step, plot)
    tags = [exahype2.api.Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag, exahype2.api.Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag]
    for anchor in time_step.find_tasks(*tags):
      update = next(anchor.tags[tag] for tag in tags if tag in anchor.tags)
      if update.solver is not self:
        continue
      task = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task(
        name   = f"{self._name}_My step after {anchor.name}",
        entity = peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.GridEntity.CELL,
      )
      task.use_cell(update.patch, peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.DataAccess.READ_AND_WRITE)
      for data in update.guard_data:
        task.use_cell(data, peano4.api.taskgraph.Task.DataAccess.READ_AND_WRITE)
      task.add_action_set(MyActionSet(update.patch, guard=update.guard))
      time_step.insert_after(anchor, task)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Drop IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag from the list if your step should
run once per time step only, i.e. not after each Runge-Kutta stage.

Declare the patch with READ_AND_WRITE even if your step only reads it:
the access mode keeps the data alive for the tasks which follow.

The routine receives the setup and the plot graph, too. The tags are only
used within the time stepping graph, though. If you want to alter the other
graphs, you have to identify the tasks of interest yourself, e.g. via their
names, or add tasks which do not depend on any existing task.


# Writing a new numerical scheme

If you write a numerical scheme of your own, i.e. a subclass of
exahype2.api.Solver.NumericalScheme, you have to keep the scheme's side of the
contract. Otherwise, add_point_wise_postprocessing() and every step which
relies on the tags will silently miss your updates. postprocess_task_graphs()
asserts that a solver with point-wise postprocessing has at least one tagged
task, but it cannot check that you have tagged all of them.

- Tag each task which writes a new solution with
  Solver.SolutionUpdateTaskTag(solver, patch, guard, guard_data).
- Tag each task which writes the input of a further stage, i.e. an
  intermediate solution, with
  Solver.IntermediateSolutionUpdateTaskTag(solver, patch, guard, guard_data).
- If the guard reads cell data besides the patch, pass these data as
  guard_data.
- Do not read the written patch in the same task. Move eigenvalue
  computations, the projection onto the faces and similar operations into
  a subsequent task.

*/
